      "content": "Premature longitudinal cracking in asphalt, especially in a hot climate like Phoenix, is often a result of a few key factors.  Thermal stresses are a big one, as the asphalt expands and contracts with the daily temperature swings, which can stress the pavement structure. Bitumen-related issues, such as aging or inadequate binder stiffness, can make the material brittle, causing it to crack under these thermal loads. You might also see issues from insufficient compaction or poor mix design, which makes the pavement more susceptible to these stresses.",
