      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The subgrade's performance is always a critical factor, even if compaction specs were met. It's less about the *curing* viscosity causing longitudinal cracks and more about how the *material properties* of the bitumen behave under those extreme heat cycles. If the bitumen is too stiff or too brittle—perhaps due to the mix design or aging—it won't flex well when the subgrade underneath is moving or reacting to temperature fluctuations. Think of it like a very stiff, brittle piece of armor trying to absorb the constant, tiny movements of a shifting, unstable ground layer.",
